{"name":"Blue Marguerite","hex":"#6a5bb1","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue marguerite","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hairy, soft, usually perennial evergreen plant of species Felicia amelloides, found along the southern coast of South Africa."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue_marguerite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Felicia amelloides","blocks":[{"text":"Felicia amelloides, the blue daisy bush, blue felicia or kingfisher daisy, is a hairy, soft, usually perennial, evergreen plant, in the family Asteraceae. It can be found along the southern coast of South Africa. It grows as groundcover, and is cultivated as an ornamental, where it was introduced in Europe in the middle of the 18th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Felicia_amelloides01.jpg/330px-Felicia_amelloides01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felicia_amelloides","wikidata":"Q354211"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wik